{"title":"ASX to open lower; Wall Street falls as the clamp tightens from rising bond yields (The Age, 2023-10-12T20:00:32+00:00)","text":"ASX to open lower; Wall Street falls as the clamp tightens from rising bond yields (The Age, 2023-10-12T20:00:32+00:00)
Loading The big jump for the 10-year Treasury yield since the summer has pulled mortgage rates to their highest levels since the turn of the millennium, and such moves could slow the economy without requiring more Fed hikes. Nothing in Thursday’s inflation report should sway the Fed one way or the other when it comes to what it will do on November 1, when it announces its next move on interest rates, said Seema Shah, chief global strategist at Principal Asset Management. She called the data “reassuringly uneventful”.

“The question around whether or not there will be one more interest rate hike is yet to be answered,” she said. Rising crude oil prices have put extra pressure on inflation since the summer, and they were volatile again on Thursday. After jumping early in the day, a barrel of benchmark US crude slipped US58¢ to settle at $US82.91. Brent crude, the international standard, rose US18¢ to $US86 a barrel.","entities":["benchmark US crude","Brent crude","ASX to open lower; Wall Street falls as the clamp tightens from rising bond yields","Thursday’s inflation report","Seema Shah","Principal Asset Management","Fed’s next move on interest rates","interest rate hike","Fed","The Age","the economy","bond yields","10-year Treasury yield","crude oil prices","ASX"],"facts":["ASX to open lower; Wall Street falls as the clamp tightens from rising bond yields -MENTIONS-> Evolution","ASX set to open higher as Wall Street rebounds; $A rises -MENTIONS-> Brent crude","ASX set to open higher as Wall Street rebounds; $A rises -MENTIONS-> benchmark US crude","ASX set to open lower as Wall Street closes September with more losses -MENTIONS-> Brent crude","ASX to open lower; Wall Street falls as the clamp tightens from rising bond yields -MENTIONS-> gold miners"]}

{"title":"Sam Bankman-Fried was a terrible boyfriend (The Verge, 2023-10-10T23:50:21+00:00)","text":"Sam Bankman-Fried was a terrible boyfriend (The Verge, 2023-10-10T23:50:21+00:00)
It was that Ellison’s calculations assumed Alameda could borrow $1.8 billion in normal dollars and $1.5 billion in crypto from FTX. The spreadsheet makes this clear with a row labeled “FTX borrows,” which Ellison said were customer funds.

Meanwhile, echoing Bankman-Fried and Ellison’s romantic relationship, FTX was keeping cozy private ties with Alameda yet publicly holding it at arm’s length. On January 14th, 2022, Bankman-Fried tweeted, “We’re launching a $2b venture fund: FTX Ventures!” Those funds came from Alameda, Ellison testified. But Bankman-Fried didn’t want to go public with the source of the funds. He said he thought Alameda’s brand was less good, and he didn’t want his name associated with it. Alameda also bought Robinhood shares for Bankman-Fried, who moved them to a vehicle called “Emergent Fidelity Technologies” to avoid association with Alameda.","entities":["crypto","Alameda","Sam Bankman-Fried was a terrible boyfriend","FTX Ventures","Emergent Fidelity Technologies","Ellison","customer funds","FTX","Sam Bankman-Fried","Robinhood","The Verge"],"facts":["SBF Trial: The latest updates from the FTX collapse’s courtroom drama -MENTIONS-> crypto","SBF, riding high on FTX, reportedly offered $5B to Trump to cancel President re-run -MENTIONS-> crypto","Is Sam Bankman-Fried a bad ‘man’ or a good ‘boy’? Lawyers swap opening statements before first witnesses take the stand -MENTIONS-> crypto","SBF’s trial starts soon, but how did he — and FTX — get here? -MENTIONS-> crypto","Is Sam Bankman-Fried’s defense even trying to win? -MENTIONS-> crypto"]}

{"title":"The jury finally hears from Sam Bankman-Fried (The Verge, 2023-10-28T00:12:41+00:00)","text":"The jury finally hears from Sam Bankman-Fried (The Verge, 2023-10-28T00:12:41+00:00)
When he found it, he was “very surprised!”

Besides the two obvious lies Bankman-Fried told on the stand — about Alameda Research’s name and about “allow_negative” — I have been struck by how little he seems to know about his own companies. Apparently, Singh, Wang, and Ellison were out there just doing whatever their little hearts desired. Because Bankman-Fried was a CEO, but definitely not the kind that pays any attention to money at his crypto trading firm and futures exchange.","entities":["The jury finally hears from Sam Bankman-Fried","Alameda Research","allow_negative","Ellison","Singh","Wang","Sam Bankman-Fried","The Verge"],"facts":["The jury finally hears from Sam Bankman-Fried -MENTIONS-> Gary Wang","The jury finally hears from Sam Bankman-Fried -MENTIONS-> Caroline Ellison","The jury finally hears from Sam Bankman-Fried -MENTIONS-> Mark Cohen","The jury finally hears from Sam Bankman-Fried -MENTIONS-> Adam Yedidia","The jury finally hears from Sam Bankman-Fried -MENTIONS-> Securities and Exchange Commission"]}

