Those privileges ultimately resulted in Alameda owing FTX $8 billion worth of customer deposits. "We gave special privileges on FTX that gave unlimited withdrawals on the platform to Alameda," Wang said. Alameda was allowed to withdraw and transfer those funds and had a $65 billion line of credit. "When customers deposited USD, it went to Alameda," he said. "It existed in the computer code. Alameda could have negative balances and unlimited withdrawals." That "bug" in the code was written by Nishad Singh, who was FTX's director of engineering, and reviewed by Wang. Bankman-Fried was calling the shots, Wang said. Wang also told the court about a $1 million personal loan he received and a $200 million to $300 million loan in his name from Alameda that was never deposited into his account, but rather was used to make investments into other companies on behalf of FTX.","entities":["Bankman-Fried","Alameda","Nishad Singh","Signal","Bahamas","FTX","Sex, Signal messages, and sabotaging FTX's code: SBF execs and Bahamas roommates tell all in court","Wang","CNBC","Code"],"facts":["SBF’s trial starts soon, but how did he — and FTX — get here? -MENTIONS-> Bankman-Fried","In the end, the FTX trial was about the friends screwed along the way -MENTIONS-> Bankman-Fried","Sam Altman backs teens’ startup, Google unveils the Pixel 8 and TikTok tests an ad-free tier -MENTIONS-> Bankman-Fried","Today the FTX jury suffered through a code review -MENTIONS-> Bankman-Fried","Bankman-Fried -SAID-> Alameda Research"]}
