A comprehensive study conducted on intelligence failures in the national arena, from the fall of the Iron Curtain in 1989 to 2010, showed no improvement in providing or thwarting warnings about strategic surprises, even if the means of gathering information had improved. Four years after signing the study, former US President Barack Obama admitted that US intelligence had not correctly assessed the severity of the threat of large parts of Iraq and Syria being occupied by ISIS.

"No matter how technologically advanced your army or how effective your intelligence gathering system, identifying strategic surprises is a human process that depends on analyzing what is called, in the intelligence community, 'weak signals,'" Barnea says. "These are pieces of relevant information that have been conveyed to the organization, but because they are low-key, or because of the information overload around them, the organization fails to identify and interpret them correctly."","entities":["Globes English | Israel Business Arena","Barnea","Israel's defense undone by reliance on technology","intelligence gathering","ISIS","fall of the Iron Curtain","US intelligence","strategic surprises","intelligence failures","weak signals","Syria","Iraq","Barack Obama","technology"],"facts":["Psagot worth 80% less since Rani Zim bought it -PUBLISHED_BY-> Globes English | Israel Business Arena",""There are no sacred numbers" -PUBLISHED_BY-> Globes English | Israel Business Arena","There are no sacred numbers -PUBLISHED_BY-> Globes English | Israel Business Arena","Office rents in Israel stay firm - for now -PUBLISHED_BY-> Globes English | Israel Business Arena","Israel's prince of public diplomacy -PUBLISHED_BY-> Globes English | Israel Business Arena"]}

